nite chain rings}, year = {2021}, eprint = {2111.04914}, note = {arXiv:2111.04914v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nQuasi-polycyclic (QP for short) codes over a finite chain ring $R$ are a generalization of quasi-cyclic codes, and these codes can be viewed as an $R[x]$-submodule of $\\mathcal{R}_m^{\\ell}$, where $\\mathcal{R}_m:= R[x]/\\langle f\\rangle$, and $f$ is a monic polynomial of degree $m$ over $R$. If $f$ factors uniquely into monic and coprime basic irreducibles, then their algebraic structure allow us to characterize the generator polynomials and the minimal generating sets of 1-generator QP codes as $R$-modules. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv Troller (https://arxiv-troller.com/?q=paper%3A+2609.35847)\nciteturn2search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; A pipeline generated with Claude Code (numerical refutation, proof by the SMT solver \\textsf{z3} from a library of lemmas, discovery of new lemmas, formalisation in \\lean 4/Mathlib) refuted $162{,}964$ conjectures and proved $117{,}390$ without any unproved hypothesis, of which $79{,}505$ are verified end to end in \\lean{} for every connected graph. ... Do Kaplansky's group-ring conjectures hold for torsion-free word-hyperbolic groups? ... As far as we know, they are the first torsion-free counterexamples to direct finiteness and to the idempotent conjecture in odd characteristic.\n\nLemmas and Conjectures in Spectral Graph Theory\n\nTristan Cazenave\n\narXiv:2611.03879\n\nAuthors: Tristan Cazenave\n\nSubmitted: Nov 2026 | Updated: Nov 2026\n\nThe corpus of Elie Duhamel and Tristan Cazenave \\cite{dc} contains $305{,}755$ automatically generated conjectures relating spectral and metric invariants of connected graphs. A pipeline generated with Claude Code (numerical refutation, proof by the SMT solver \\textsf{z3} from a library of lemmas, discovery of new lemmas, formalisation in \\lean 4/Mathlib) refuted $162{,}964$ conjectures and proved $